    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Grip size is the spec almost nobody checks and almost everybody feels. A handle that is too thin makes you clamp down to stop the paddle turning, and that constant squeeze is behind a surprising amount of forearm and elbow soreness. A handle that is too thick blunts your wrist and costs you spin. The fix takes two minutes with a ruler, and it narrows your paddle shortlist faster than any other measurement.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Grip Size Matters More Than It Seems
          </h2>

          <p>
            The grip is your only connection to the paddle, so its size shapes every shot. When the circumference suits your hand, you can hold the paddle with a relaxed grip pressure and let the wrist work freely for dinks, flicks and serves. When it does not, your hand compensates, and that compensation shows up as tension, fatigue and lost touch.
          </p>

          <p>
            A grip that is too small is the more common and more harmful mistake. Because the handle is thin, the paddle tends to rotate in your hand on off-centre hits, so you instinctively squeeze harder to hold it steady. That extra squeeze loads the forearm tendons that run to the elbow, which is why undersized grips are frequently mentioned as a contributor to tennis elbow among racket and paddle players.
          </p>

          <p>
            A grip that is too large causes different problems. Your fingers cannot wrap fully around the handle, which reduces control and limits wrist movement. You lose some of the snap that generates spin on serves and roll volleys, and quick hand exchanges at the kitchen feel slower. Oversized grips are less likely to cause pain, but they cost you finesse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the fix is cheap and simple, grip size is worth settling before you compare anything else. It sits second only to weight in the order we recommend in <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">how to choose a pickleball paddle</a>, and unlike face material or core thickness it is partly adjustable after you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Common Grip Sizes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most pickleball paddles have a grip circumference between about 4 and 4.5 inches. The most common size is around 4.25 inches, which suits a broad range of adult hands, and many manufacturers offer only one grip size per model. Thinner grips around 4 inches are often found on paddles aimed at players who like a lot of wrist action, while thicker grips around 4.5 inches appear on some models aimed at players with larger hands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because many paddles come in a single grip size, it pays to check the circumference on the listing before you buy rather than assuming it will fit. If a paddle you like is only available with a grip slightly smaller than you need, that is usually fine, because you can build it up. If it is only available with a grip that is too large, there is no practical way to reduce it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below gives a rough starting point. Hand size varies widely between people of the same height, so treat height-based guidance as a fallback and use the measuring method in the next section whenever you can.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grip circumference</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rough fit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical player</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 4 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small hands, wrist-heavy players</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Players who want maximum wrist action and spin</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 4.125 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small to medium hands</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many women and juniors; players who build up with an overgrip</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 4.25 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium hands (most common)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The broad default for most adult players</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 4.375 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium to large hands</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Players who prefer a fuller handle</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 4.5 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large hands</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Players with long fingers who dislike thin handles</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            As a very rough guide sometimes quoted by retailers, players under about 5 feet 2 inches often suit grips around 4 inches, players between about 5 feet 3 inches and 5 feet 11 inches often suit around 4.25 inches, and taller players often suit around 4.5 inches. Hand size does not track height closely, so use this only if you cannot measure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Measure Your Hand
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most widely used method needs only a ruler. Open your hitting hand flat with the fingers together. Find the middle crease of your palm, the line that runs across the palm roughly in the middle. Measure from that crease up to the tip of your ring finger. The distance in inches roughly corresponds to the grip circumference to look for: about 4 inches suggests a 4-inch grip, about 4.25 inches suggests a 4.25-inch grip, and so on.
          </p>

          <p>
            A second check uses a paddle you already own or one in a shop. Hold the paddle in a continental grip, the handshake grip used for most pickleball shots. There should be a small gap between the tips of your fingers and the base of your palm, roughly wide enough to fit the index finger of your other hand. If your fingertips dig into your palm, the grip is too small. If there is a large gap and you cannot fit your finger snugly, the grip is too large.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure both methods if you can, and if they disagree slightly, trust the finger test on a real handle, because it accounts for the shape of the grip as well as its circumference. Paddle handles are not perfectly round; many are slightly octagonal or rectangular, and two grips listed at the same circumference can feel a touch different.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write your size down once you have it. It saves time every time you shop, and it lets you filter listings quickly. If you play with a glove or in humid conditions where you use a tacky overgrip, factor that into the fit, since both change how the handle feels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="between" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Between Sizes: Go Small
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your measurement falls between two sizes, choose the smaller one. This is the standard advice across racket and paddle sports for a simple reason: you can increase a grip's circumference by adding an overgrip or a thicker replacement grip, but you cannot easily make a handle thinner. A slightly small grip plus an overgrip lands you close to the size you actually need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each overgrip typically adds about a sixteenth of an inch to the circumference. So a 4.125-inch grip with one overgrip ends up close to 4.25 inches, and two overgrips add roughly an eighth of an inch. That gives you fine control over the final fit, which is more precise than the half-size steps paddles are sold in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choosing small also suits the way pickleball is played. Much of the game happens at the kitchen, where wrist action on dinks, flicks and speed-ups matters. A slightly slimmer grip makes those shots easier, as long as it is not so small that you squeeze to hold it. If you find yourself gripping tightly after building up the handle, add another overgrip rather than living with the tension.
          </p>

          <p>
            The exception is a player who already suffers from elbow or forearm pain and finds a slightly fuller grip lets them relax. In that case, a grip at or just above your measured size, rather than below it, may be more comfortable. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-tennis-elbow/">paddles for tennis elbow</a> guide covers grip choice alongside paddle weight and core softness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overgrips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Overgrips and Replacement Grips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Overgrips are thin, tacky wraps applied over the existing grip. They add a small amount of circumference, absorb sweat, and improve tackiness, which helps you hold the paddle with less pressure. Many players replace their overgrip regularly as it wears smooth, which keeps the feel consistent and is far cheaper than replacing the paddle grip itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement grips are thicker and replace the paddle's original grip entirely. They are useful when the factory grip has worn out or when you want a different feel, such as more cushioning. Cushioned replacement grips can make the paddle feel softer and may absorb some vibration, which some players with sore arms appreciate, though they also add circumference and weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you add an overgrip, wrap it with a consistent overlap and finish it neatly at the top with the supplied tape. An uneven wrap creates ridges that change how the handle sits in your hand. Start at the butt cap and work upward, keeping light tension so the wrap lies flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that any grip material also adds a little weight near the handle, which can shift the paddle's balance slightly toward the hand. For most players the change is negligible, but if you have tuned your paddle carefully with lead tape, as described in our <a href="/pickleball-paddle-weight/">paddle weight guide</a>, re-check the feel after a grip change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Handle Length and Two-Handed Backhands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grip circumference is only half the fit. Handle length decides how much room you have for your hand, and it matters most for players who hit two-handed backhands or who like to slide their hand up and down the handle. Standard handles are typically around 5 inches long, while long-handle paddles often run to roughly 5.5 inches or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hit a two-handed backhand, a longer handle is close to essential. On a short handle, the top hand ends up partly on the paddle face, which interferes with contact and makes the shot awkward. Players coming from tennis, where two-handed backhands are common, often prefer long handles for this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is face size. Pickleball rules limit the combined length and width of the paddle, so a longer handle generally means a shorter or narrower hitting surface. That is why many long-handle paddles are also elongated in shape, and why their sweet spot can be smaller. Our <a href="/elongated-vs-standard-pickleball-paddle/">elongated vs standard paddle</a> guide explains how shape and handle length interact.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hit one-handed and stay compact at the kitchen, a standard handle gives you more face and a larger sweet spot, which is usually the better trade. If you are unsure, hold a standard and a long-handle paddle side by side and try a two-handed grip on each; the right length is usually obvious within a few swings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Checking the Fit in Your Hand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have a paddle, confirm the fit in play rather than relying on measurements alone. Hold the paddle in a relaxed continental grip and hit a few dinks and volleys. You should be able to keep a light grip pressure, roughly firm enough to control the paddle without white knuckles, and the paddle should not twist noticeably on slightly off-centre hits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Signs that the grip is too small include squeezing hard, a paddle that turns in your hand, and forearm fatigue after a session. Signs that it is too large include difficulty generating spin, a feeling that the wrist is locked, and fingers that do not wrap fully around the handle. Either way, adjust with overgrips if you can, or consider a different paddle if the mismatch is large.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check again after a few weeks. Grips compress and overgrips wear smooth, so a fit that felt right at first can loosen over time. Replacing the overgrip restores both the size and the tackiness, and it is one of the cheapest ways to keep a paddle feeling new.
          </p>

          <p>
            With grip size settled, you can compare paddles on the specs that differ between models. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> list notes grip circumference and handle length for every pick, so you can filter out the ones that will not fit before you compare cores and faces.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Measure, size down, build up.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Measure from the middle palm crease to the tip of your ring finger, choose the nearest size or the one below it, and add overgrips until the paddle sits securely with a relaxed grip.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never checking grip size at all</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many players buy a paddle on looks or reviews and only notice the grip after weeks of forearm fatigue. Measuring your hand takes two minutes with a ruler and tells you which paddles to rule out. Check the circumference on every listing before buying, especially for models sold in only one grip size.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a grip that is too large</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Players sometimes size up thinking a fuller handle will be more comfortable, but an oversized grip locks the wrist and costs spin and touch at the kitchen. Because you cannot shrink a handle, a grip that is too large is harder to fix than one that is slightly small. When in doubt, size down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Squeezing a small grip instead of building it up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a paddle feels slightly thin, the answer is an overgrip, not a tighter hand. Constant squeezing loads the forearm tendons and is a common contributor to elbow soreness. Add an overgrip, about a sixteenth of an inch at a time, until you can hold the paddle with a relaxed grip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring handle length for two-handed backhands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard handle leaves too little room for two hands, so the top hand ends up on the face. If you hit two-handed backhands, choose a long-handle paddle of roughly 5.5 inches or more. The trade is a smaller face, so one-handed players should usually stay with a standard handle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting overgrips wear out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overgrips lose tackiness and compress over time, which changes both feel and size. A worn overgrip encourages a tighter grip and can make the paddle slip on sweaty days. Replace overgrips regularly; it is cheap, quick, and keeps the handle feeling as it did when you first sized it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grip size pickleball paddle do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure from the middle crease of your palm to the tip of your ring finger. That distance in inches roughly matches the grip circumference to look for. Most adults fall between about 4 and 4.5 inches, with 4.25 inches the most common. If you are between sizes, choose the smaller grip and build it up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the standard pickleball grip size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 4.25 inches is the most common grip circumference and suits a wide range of adult hands. Many paddles are sold in only this size. Smaller grips around 4 inches and larger grips around 4.5 inches are available on some models for players whose hands fall outside the middle range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does an overgrip add to grip size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An overgrip typically adds about a sixteenth of an inch to the circumference. Two overgrips add roughly an eighth of an inch. That lets you fine-tune a slightly small grip to the exact fit you want, which is why sizing down and building up is the standard advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to have a smaller or larger pickleball grip?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slightly smaller is usually better, because you can build a small grip up with overgrips but cannot reduce a large one. A smaller grip also allows more wrist action at the kitchen. The grip should not be so small that you squeeze to control it, though, because that can strain the forearm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can the wrong grip size cause tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A grip that is too small is commonly cited as a contributing factor, because it makes players squeeze harder, loading the forearm tendons. It is rarely the only cause, since technique, volume of play and paddle choice also matter. If you have persistent pain, check your grip and seek advice from a medical professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a long handle pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you hit two-handed backhands or like to move your hand along the handle. Long handles of roughly 5.5 inches or more give room for two hands. One-handed players usually do better with a standard handle of about 5 inches, which leaves more room for the hitting face.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my pickleball grip is too small?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Signs include squeezing hard to control the paddle, the paddle twisting in your hand on off-centre hits, and forearm fatigue after play. With a continental grip, your fingertips may press into your palm. Adding an overgrip usually fixes a grip that is only slightly small.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

